  "detail": null,
  "summary": "Your messages queue is empty. I worked through 7 refill messages for 3 patients under the refill protocol (RBC-RX-05). Three results and one message came in while I was working. Two of the results reversed lisinopril refills I had already placed, so I cancelled those (nothing had been dispensed). The third result let me lengthen a short refill. Two patients' lisinopril is now waiting for your review.\n\n**Frank Odum (pt-0027), lisinopril 10 mg – not refilled, needs your review**\n- **First decision:** The St. Brigid potassium of 5.9 from 8/27 didn't count because the sample was hemolyzed (damaged). On his last valid potassium (4.5, 1/20) I renewed for 90 days with 1 refill and ordered a repeat BMP (sr-t0002).\n- **What changed:** A new St. Brigid BMP from 10/20 arrived on a good sample, showing potassium 5.8. At 5.5 or higher the protocol says no refill.\n- **Done:** I cancelled the refill (mr-t0001). His previous order (mr-00283) was stopped when I placed the refill, so no lisinopril order is active. He told us he has no refills left.\n- **Your task:** task-t0021, marked urgent, due 10/26. The repeat BMP order is still open for you to keep or cancel.\n- He was sent a corrected message with warning symptoms to watch for. His hernia repair is set for 11/12.\n\n**Samuel Price (pt-0030)**\n- **Metformin 1000 mg twice daily – renewed** for 90 days with 1 refill (mr-t0004). His eGFR was 65, corrected today to 58; both pass. His last clinic HbA1c (3/30) was too old, but a scanned outside HbA1c of 7.1% from 8/19 counts. The patient's request and both pharmacy requests (one arrived while I worked) got this one order.\n- **Lisinopril 20 mg – not refilled, needs your review.** I first renewed it, since potassium was 5.2 and creatinine was up 24% after the 9/18 dose increase. Today the lab corrected the 10/6 creatinine from 1.36 to 1.49. That is 35% above the 1.10 before the increase, which the protocol treats like a potassium of 5.5 or higher. I cancelled the refill (mr-t0003); his previous order (mr-00295) is also stopped.\n- **Your task:** task-t0022, marked urgent, due 10/26. He was sent a corrected message.\n\n**Marcus Webb (pt-0031) – both renewed**\n- **Metformin 500 mg twice daily:** 90 days with 1 refill (mr-t0007). I did not use the 10/14 eGFR of 39, which was entered in error (wrong patient's sample). His valid eGFR was 92, and HbA1c was 6.9% on 9/28.\n- **Lisinopril 10 mg:** His last BMP (10/20/2025) was just over 12 months old, so I first gave a 30-day supply and ordered a BMP. A valid BMP from 10/22 then arrived (potassium 4.6). I replaced the 30-day order with 90 days and 1 refill (mr-t0020).\n- **Your task:** I can't cancel lab orders, so I sent you a routine task (task-t0023) to cancel the BMP order I placed (sr-t0006), which is now a duplicate.\n- He was sent an updated message.\n\nEach patient got a reply, and each chart has a note explaining the decisions and why they changed. All the queue messages and the four items that arrived while I worked are closed.\n\n**Outside this job:** Your results queue still has 8 older items for other patients. These include critical potassiums for pt-0016 (6.3) and pt-0008 (6.4), plus CT reports for pt-0006 and pt-0007. I didn't touch them because they're outside the messages queue and the protocol only lets me act for patients in that queue. They need prompt attention.",
